body:
After #1784 merged and closed #1780, two recovery boundaries remain in `bf2a1f10bb0eb68eaea19c089d3ebc04db2346df`. This tracks the follow-up to [the regression tests on #1784](https://github.com/opensandbox-group/OpenSandbox/pull/1784#issuecomment-5599477207), with an additional real-filesystem failure case. I found no open implementation in the current upper/reclaim/stale searches.

### Reproduction and impact

`NewIsolatedRunner → NewUpperManager → reclaimStale` classifies every child containing an `upper/` directory as an old session, then recursively removes it. Failed removals are kept in memory for `CollectWithErrors`. `Remove` and `CollectWithErrors` also delete the whole session parent.

- An unrelated `shared-data/upper/important.bin` is deleted during construction. Checking the allocator's 32-hex-character ID format would prevent that example, but cannot distinguish an unrelated directory with the same name/layout from a legacy session.
- Allocate a real pair, write `work/residue.bin`, chmod work to `0500`, then construct another manager as a non-root UID. `RemoveAll` removes upper but fails on the work file. Restore permissions and restart again: the work-only residue is no longer recognized. GC on the original manager succeeds, so this is loss of the retry record across restart.
- **An empty parent can also remain.** After normal allocation, chmod `upper_root` to `0500`. Startup can delete everything inside the session but cannot unlink the session directory from its parent. After restoring root permissions and restarting, the now-empty allocated directory is skipped forever. Adding `work/` to the predicate does not cover this case. A probe-only marker placed inside the session directory was also deleted by the same `RemoveAll` before it failed.

On Linux, an unprivileged UID, and Go 1.25.9 at the merged commit, each of these failure expectations reproduced 10/10 times with `-race`. The allocator-shaped unrelated-directory expectation also failed 10/10; that case demonstrates a migration ambiguity rather than an adversarial ownership claim. Same-manager work-only and empty-directory controls each passed 10/10. All 16 existing UpperManager tests pass; the extra files pass gofmt and `go vet` with the overlay.

This affects upper-root data preservation and startup/GC recovery. It does not establish a cross-tenant exploit, mount-teardown ordering, or a hard-quota guarantee. The empty-directory case leaks a directory, not payload bytes; the work-only case can retain payload. Missing upper directories contribute zero to the existing upper-byte counter.

### Proposed scope and tradeoff

The cleanup record should remain discoverable until deletion of the entire session parent succeeds, across startup, explicit removal, and periodic GC.

A possible durable design is a reserved registry outside each removable session subtree, recording allocated IDs before publishing the directories and removing the record only after successful cleanup. It needs explicit handling of allocation rollback, record-only crash states, and stale records after directory deletion. A marker inside the recursively removed subtree is insufficient with the current removal operation. Another option is a cleanup tombstone/rename protocol, but its namespace, collisions, and recovery rules also need defining.

The legacy rule needs agreement before a production patch:

- Treat a configured root as exclusively execd-owned and reclaim recognizable legacy residues; document the migration and shared-root incompatibility explicitly.
- Preserve ambiguous unmarked legacy data in shared roots and introduce durable ownership for new allocations, with a deliberate migration/reconciliation path for old data.
- Narrow matching to allocator IDs plus upper/work layout as an interim mitigation. This cannot provide the current unconditional shared-root preservation guarantee and still misses empty residue.

I prefer durable cleanup records with an explicit legacy policy. This issue is limited to recovery identity and retry persistence; changing live-session admission, hard quota enforcement (#1773), or full process/mount teardown is outside its scope. Existing author work on #1784 remains credited; this is a post-merge follow-up, not a competing implementation.

### Evaluated interim alternative

I also tested a local Go overlay that restricts discovery to `^(?:[0-9a-f]{32}|fallback-[1-9][0-9]*)$` and requires either an `upper` or `work` directory. It reuses the existing cleanup operation; no production file was changed or submitted.

| Regression expectation | Current merged main | ID + upper/work overlay |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Preserve `shared-data/upper` | Fails 10/10 | Passes 10/10 |
| Recover work-only residue after restart | Fails 10/10 | Passes 10/10 |
| Recover empty allocated parent after restart | Fails 10/10 | Fails 10/10 |
| Preserve unrelated allocator-shaped name/layout | Fails 10/10 | Fails 10/10 |

All 16 existing UpperManager tests pass with the overlay, as does package vet. That demonstrates why passing the current suite or broadening the layout predicate is not enough. The overlay is an evaluated, incomplete alternative, **not a proposed ready-to-merge fix**.

The unmodified merged commit also passes 420 top-level tests with 2 skips using Linux / Go 1.25.9:

```sh
go test -race -count=1 -timeout=180s ./pkg/isolation ./pkg/runtime ./pkg/sessionresource ./pkg/telemetry ./pkg/web/controller
go vet ./pkg/isolation ./pkg/runtime ./pkg/sessionresource ./pkg/telemetry ./pkg/web/controller
```

Skipped: `TestBwrapLifecycleEndToEnd` needs the installed native session gate; `TestHardeningKeepCapabilities` needs root capabilities. I did not install privileged helpers or elevate the UID. An initial sandboxed attempt was blocked by local socket/credential-option restrictions; the full rerun above used the existing unprivileged account outside that sandbox. This validates the existing callers on this host, not a recovery fix, privileged overlay mounts, or Pod restarts.

### Validation plan

Keep the original unrelated-directory and work-only regressions; add the empty-parent regression below. For the selected design, inject interruption/error at record creation, directory creation, partial removal, complete removal, and record retirement. Check constructor recovery, explicit `Remove`, repeated `CollectWithErrors`, live-entry preservation, allocation after cleanup, and the runtime/sessionresource callers. Use real filesystem permission failures as well as deterministic injection. Verify touched-file formatting/type checks and package vet/race tests. Privileged overlay and Pod restart testing must be reported separately.

The commands below are reproduction commands: the regression command is expected to fail on current main. There is no production fix or successful PR CI claimed here.

```sh
# Place both files from the linked comment and the test below in pkg/isolation.
go test -race -count=10 -run '^Test(ReclaimReview|RecoveryBoundary)' ./pkg/isolation
go test -race -count=1 -run '^Test(NewUpperManager|UpperManager)' ./pkg/isolation
go vet ./pkg/isolation
```

<details>
<summary>Additional runnable boundary tests</summary>

```go
package isolation

import (
	"errors"
	"os"
	"path/filepath"
	"testing"
)

// A real RemoveAll can empty a session directory yet fail to unlink it from
// an unwritable parent. A cleanup marker inside that directory is lost too.
func TestRecoveryBoundaryEmptyResidue(t *testing.T) {
	if os.Geteuid() == 0 {
		t.Skip("requires an unprivileged UID")
	}
	for _, restart := range []bool{false, true} {
		name := "same-manager"
		if restart {
			name = "after-restart"
		}
		t.Run(name, func(t *testing.T) {
			root := t.TempDir()
			original, err := NewUpperManager(root, 1024)
			if err != nil {
				t.Fatal(err)
			}
			id, upper, _, err := original.Allocate()
			if err != nil {
				t.Fatal(err)
			}
			parent := filepath.Dir(upper)
			marker := filepath.Join(parent, ".execd-owned")
			if err := os.WriteFile(marker, []byte("probe-only ownership marker"), 0o600); err != nil {
				t.Fatal(err)
			}
			if err := os.Chmod(root, 0o500); err != nil {
				t.Fatal(err)
			}
			t.Cleanup(func() { _ = os.Chmod(root, 0o700) })
			first, err := NewUpperManager(root, 1024)
			if err != nil {
				t.Fatal(err)
			}
			children, err := os.ReadDir(parent)
			if err != nil {
				t.Fatal(err)
			}
			if len(children) != 0 {
				t.Fatalf("expected an empty residue, got %v", children)
			}
			if _, err := os.Stat(marker); !errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {
				t.Fatalf("expected RemoveAll to remove the internal marker: %v", err)
			}
			if entry := first.entries[id]; entry == nil || entry.InUse {
				t.Fatal("first manager must retain retry entry")
			}
			if err := os.Chmod(root, 0o700); err != nil {
				t.Fatal(err)
			}
			retry := first
			if restart {
				retry, err = NewUpperManager(root, 1024)
				if err != nil {
					t.Fatal(err)
				}
			}
			freed, err := retry.CollectWithErrors()
			if err != nil {
				t.Fatal(err)
			}
			t.Logf("restart=%v tracked=%d freed=%v", restart, len(retry.entries), freed)
			if _, err := os.Stat(parent); !errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {
				t.Fatalf("empty allocated directory lost across restart: %v", err)
			}
		})
	}
}

// An allocator-shaped name does not distinguish unrelated data from a
// legacy session. This pins down the shared-root migration tradeoff.
func TestRecoveryBoundaryPreservesAllocatorShapedUnrelatedData(t *testing.T) {
	root := t.TempDir()
	id := "00112233445566778899aabbccddeeff"
	payload := filepath.Join(root, id, "upper", "important.bin")
	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(payload), 0o755); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Join(root, id, "work"), 0o755); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if err := os.WriteFile(payload, []byte("never allocated by execd"), 0o600); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if _, err := NewUpperManager(root, 1024); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if _, err := os.Stat(payload); err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("allocator-shaped unrelated data was removed: %v", err)
	}
}
```

</details>

## Why do you need it?

The generated Server OpenAPI describes HTTP Proxy responses as JSON-only `200` plus validation `422`, although the proxy forwards the sandbox service's status, body, and Content-Type.

Reproduced on `main@1eb8fffa0493fdfae4cf021580cdff61c9d1f50b` with locked FastAPI 0.137.2 and Python 3.10.20. All 20 HTTP operations are affected: root and `/v1` aliases, root and full-path routes, and GET/POST/PUT/DELETE/PATCH.

Existing proxy route tests already demonstrate forwarded `201` and `302` responses. A sandbox service can return HTML, plain text, binary data, or its own error response. These are normal proxy outcomes, but the generated schema lacks a default response and advertises only JSON for `200`.

### Reproduction

From the commit above, with Server development dependencies installed:

```sh
cd server
SANDBOX_CONFIG_PATH=tests/testdata/config.toml uv run python - <<'PY'
import runpy

# Use the repository fixture configuration and mocked Docker client.
app = runpy.run_path("tests/conftest.py")["app"]
for path, item in app.openapi()["paths"].items():
    if "/proxy/{port}" in path:
        for method, operation in item.items():
            print(method.upper(), path, operation["responses"])
PY
```

Every operation reports:

```json
{
  "200": {
    "description": "Successful Response",
    "content": {"application/json": {"schema": {}}}
  },
  "422": {
    "description": "Validation Error",
    "content": {
      "application/json": {
        "schema": {"$ref": "#/components/schemas/HTTPValidationError"}
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## How could it be?

Keep the routes visible in generated OpenAPI and describe their existing transport behavior:

- Explicit `200` and `default` responses with `content: {"*/*": {}}`.
- No fixed payload model for sandbox service responses.
- Preserve the explicit Server validation `422` declaration.
- Preserve authentication, runtime routing, response forwarding, WebSocket behavior, and current operation IDs.

The server can still reject requests before forwarding; the proposed metadata does not redefine those errors or wrap backend failures in the lifecycle ErrorResponse model.

A minimal fix can add shared metadata to the existing method-specific schema routes, use StreamingResponse as their documented response class, and leave hidden runtime dispatch routes untouched. FastAPI's `openapi_extra` applies after automatic validation responses are generated, preserving `422` while adding `default`.

## Other related information

[#1632](https://github.com/opensandbox-group/OpenSandbox/issues/1632) originally reported this gap. It was closed immediately after [#1634](https://github.com/opensandbox-group/OpenSandbox/pull/1634) merged. That PR addressed the separate operation-ID problem, and its description explicitly left response metadata out of scope. The response gap remains reproducible on the commit above.

[#1631](https://github.com/opensandbox-group/OpenSandbox/pull/1631) changes proxy target resolution; its merged diff does not add response schema metadata.

This concerns generated Server OpenAPI. `specs/sandbox-lifecycle.yml` defines endpoint discovery but does not define these proxy transport paths; no SDK generation or lifecycle contract change is proposed.

## 背景

我们正在使用 OpenSandbox/Aone Sandbox 接入基于 Node.js、TypeScript 和 pi 的 Agent Server。

目前已有 Python、Java/Kotlin、Go SDK，但暂未发现官方 Node.js/TypeScript SDK。自行封装 OpenAPI 会增加开发和维护成本。

## 期望能力

希望 Node.js/TypeScript SDK 支持：

- 创建、连接、续期和销毁 Sandbox；
- 查询状态及健康信息；
- 执行和中断命令；
- 文件上传、下载和读写；
- 网络 Egress 配置；
- Sandbox Extensions；
- `spiffe.lazyAuth` 和 `attachSpiffeIdentity`；
- 日志及诊断信息查询。

## 示例

```ts
const sandbox = await Sandbox.create({
  template: "code-interpreter",
  extensions: {
    "spiffe.lazyAuth": "true",
  },
});

await sandbox.attachSpiffeIdentity({
  rawEmpID: "123456",
  agentID: "spiffe://example/agent/123",
  agentToken: "<initial-token>",
});

const result = await sandbox.commands.run({
  command: ["echo", "hello"],
});

await sandbox.kill();

